ENDPOINT SECURITY MONITORING USING WINDOWS DEFENDER

1. Introduction

Nowadays, computers and laptops are exposed to many different cyber threats such as viruses, malware, spyware, ransomware, and other harmful programs. Because of this, protecting the endpoint device is an important part of cybersecurity.

Windows has a built-in security application called Windows Security, which includes Microsoft Defender Antivirus. It helps protect the computer by checking files and applications, providing real-time protection, detecting threats, and performing security scans.

2. Aim

To study and perform basic endpoint security monitoring using Windows Defender by checking the security settings.

3. Objectives

The main objectives of this practical are:

- To understand the basic concept of endpoint security.
- To learn about Microsoft Defender Antivirus.
- To check whether real-time protection is enabled.
- To perform a security scan on a Windows computer.
- To check the protection history for detected threats.
- To check the Windows Defender Firewall status.
- To use PowerShell to check Defender's security status.
- To understand how Windows Defender helps protect a computer.


4. Tools Required

Tool| Purpose
Windows 10/11 Computer:- Used as the endpoint device
Windows Security:- Used to manage security settings
Microsoft Defender Antivirus:- Used for malware protection
Windows Defender Firewall:-Used for network protection
PowerShell:-Used to check Defender status
Internet Connection:- Used for security and definition updates


5. What is Endpoint Security?

Endpoint security means protecting devices such as computers, laptops, and workstations from cyber threats.

These devices can become an entry point for attackers if they are not properly protected. Endpoint security helps detect harmful programs, prevent unauthorized activities, and protect important files and information.

In this practical, Windows Defender is used to monitor the security condition of a Windows computer.

The basic process is:

Endpoint → Monitoring → Threat Detection → Alert → Investigation → Response


6. What is Microsoft Defender?

Microsoft Defender Antivirus is the antivirus protection that comes with Windows. It checks files, applications, and other activities on the computer to identify possible threats.

It provides features such as real-time protection, malware scanning, threat detection, and quarantine of detected harmful files.

7. Windows Security

Windows Security is the application used to access the main security features of Windows.

Some of its important sections are:

Virus & threat protection

This section is used to check antivirus protection and perform different types of scans.

Firewall & network protection

This section shows the status of the Windows Firewall and provides information about network protection.

App & browser control

This helps protect the computer from potentially unsafe applications, files, and websites.

Device security

This section provides information about the security features available on the computer's hardware.

Device performance & health

This provides information about the general health and performance of the Windows device.


8. Real-Time Protection

Real-time protection is one of the important features of Microsoft Defender.

It continuously checks files, applications, and activities while the computer is being used. If Defender finds something suspicious, it can warn the user and take protective action.

9. Security Scanning

Windows Defender provides different options for scanning the computer.

Quick Scan

A Quick Scan checks the common areas of the computer where threats are more likely to be found. It is useful when we want to perform a quick security check.

Full Scan

A Full Scan checks the computer more thoroughly. Since it checks more files and locations, it usually takes more time.

Custom Scan

A Custom Scan can be used when we want to check a particular file, folder, or location.

In this practical, a Quick Scan is performed to check the current security condition of the computer.


10. Protection History

Protection History shows the security actions taken by Windows Defender.

It can show information about detected threats, blocked files, potentially unwanted applications, and other security-related actions.

Checking Protection History helps the user understand whether Defender has recently detected or blocked anything suspicious.

11. Windows Defender Firewall

Windows Defender Firewall helps protect the computer from unauthorized network connections.

It monitors network traffic going into and out of the computer and applies the configured firewall rules.

Checking the firewall status is important because antivirus protection alone is not enough. 


12. PowerShell Monitoring

PowerShell can also be used to check the status of Microsoft Defender.

The following command is used:

Get-MpComputerStatus

After running the command, information about Defender's services and protection features is displayed.

This provides a simple way to check whether important Defender components are running properly.


